These results were derived in Buchmuller et al. (2021) based on the loop number densities in the so-called BOS model (Blanco-Pillado et al., 2014), which explains the occurrence of factors such as 0.27, 0.45, and 0.31 in Eqs. (C22) and (C23) (see Buchmuller et al. (2021) for more details).

Finally, the expressions in Eqs. (C20), (C21), and (C22) enable us to estimate the total number densities of _all_ generations of segments that descend from closed loops breaking apart because of monopole nucleation. In principle, these number densities are described by a partial integro-differential equation where the abundance of the nth segment generation acts as a source term for the (n+1)th generation. Formally, this partial integro-differential equation can be solved analytically in terms of an infinite recursive series (Buchmuller et al., 2021). The numerical evaluation of this series is, however, technically demanding, which is why we choose to follow a different approach for the purposes of this paper. As shown in Buchmuller et al. (2021), it turns out that the total number densities for segments from closed loops result in predictions for the GW spectrum that are very similar to those obtained from the corresponding first-generation number densities in Eqs. (C20), (C21), and (C22) -- up to a numerical fudge factor of \\mathcal{O}\\left(10\\right). At the level of the number densities, it therefore suffices to rescale all first-generation number densities by a constant factor in order to obtain effective number densities for all generations of segments that descend from closed loops breaking apart,

\\tilde{n}_{s}^{\\rm rr}\\left(\\ell,t\\right)\\rightarrow\\text{fudge}\\times\\tilde{n }_{s,1}^{\\rm rr}\\left(\\ell,t\\right)\\,,\\qquad\\tilde{n}_{s}^{\\rm rm}\\left(\\ell,t \\right)\\rightarrow\\text{fudge}\\times\\tilde{n}_{s,1}^{\\rm rm}\\left(\\ell,t \\right)\\,,\\qquad\\tilde{n}_{s}^{\\rm mm}\\left(\\ell,t\\right)\\rightarrow\\text{fudge }\\times\\tilde{n}_{s,1}^{\\rm mm}\\left(\\ell,t\\right)\\,. (C25)

We stress that the functional dependence on \\ell and t is typically not the same for the first-generation and total number densities; the rescaling in Eq. (C25), however, achieves comparable results at the level of the GW spectrum. In our analysis, we consistently use a fudge factor of 5, which is a characteristic value across large regions of parameter space.

### Domain walls

In Fig. 31, we report the posterior distribution for all the parameters of the domain wall models, including the spectral shape parameters b and c that were excluded from Fig. 12. For both the dw-sm and dw-dr models we find a flat posterior for the high-frequency slope, b. This result is expected since most of the low frequency bins are fit by the low-frequency tail of the spectrum. For the width parameter, c, instead, both models prefer values near the upper range of our prior range. This preference for wider spectra derives from a mismatch between the reconstructed slope of the GWB, a\\sim 2 and the one predicted by causality, a\\sim 3. Larger width parameters make the spectrum from domain walls flatter near the peak and allow for a better fit to the data.

### Dark matter substructures

In this appendix we provide more details on the procedure that we followed to derive the constraints on f_{\\text{\\tiny PBH}} reported in Section 6.2.

Given a PBH with position relative to the pulsar given by \\mathbf{r}(\\mathbf{t})=\\mathbf{r_{0}}+\\mathbf{v}\\mathbf{t}, where \\mathbf{r_{0}} and \\mathbf{v} are the initial PBH position and velocity, respectively, we can write the Doppler and Shapiro signals as

h_{D}(t) =\\frac{GM}{v^{2}}\\hat{\\mathbf{d}}\\cdot\\left(\\sqrt{1+x_{D}^{2}}\\hat{ \\mathbf{b}}_{D}-\\sinh^{-1}(x)\\hat{\\mathbf{v}}\\right) (C26) h_{S}(t) =2GM\\log(1+x_{S}^{2})\\,, (C27) where x_{\\rm D}\\equiv(t-t_{\\rm D,0})/\\tau_{\\rm D} and x_{\\rm S}\\equiv(t-t_{\\rm S,0})/\\tau_{\\rm S}. These expressions only include cubic terms in time t and have been previously derived in (Dror et al., 2019; Lee et al., 2021). For the static limit in which \\tau\\gg T_{\\rm obs}, these expressions can be expanded in series of t_{0}/\\tau. Since the \\mathcal{O}(t^{2}) terms would be degenerate with the timing model, the measurable new-physics signal can then be parametrized as a term \\propto t^{3} as

h(t)_{\\rm D(S)}=\\frac{A_{\\rm D~{}(S),~{}sta}}{\\rm yr^{2}}t^{3}\\,, (C28) where A_{\\rm D(S),~{}sta} for both the Doppler and Shapiro static signal cases are dimensionless amplitudes given by Figure 31: Same as Fig. 12 but including the spectral shape parameters b, c and the SMBHB parameters A_{\\rm BHB} and \\gamma_{\\rm BHB}.

A_{\\rm D,\\;stat} = {\\rm yr}^{2}\\frac{GM}{2v^{2}}\\hat{\\mathbf{d}}\\cdot\\left[\\frac{t_{D,0}}{ \\tau_{D}^{4}}\\frac{1}{(1+t_{D,0}^{2}/\\tau_{D}^{2})^{5/2}}\\hat{\\mathbf{b}}_{D}+\\frac {1}{3\\tau_{D}^{3}}\\frac{1-2t_{D,0}^{2}/\\tau_{D}^{2}}{(1+t_{D,0}^{2}/\\tau_{D}^{2 })^{5/2}}\\hat{\\mathbf{v}}\\right] (C29) A_{\\rm S,\\;stat} = -{\\rm yr}^{2}\\frac{4GM}{3}\\frac{t_{S,0}}{\\tau_{S}^{4}}\\frac{3-t_{S,0}^{2}/\\tau_{S}^{2}}{(1+t_{S,0}^{2}/\\tau_{S}^{2})^{3}}\\,. (C30)

For the Doppler case, in the dynamic limit when \\tau\\ll T_{\\rm obs}, the dominant contribution would come from the first term in Eq. (C26) where \\sqrt{1+x_{D}^{2}}\\propto|t-t_{0}|. Upto linear order in x_{D}, we can write

h_{D,\\rm dyn}(t)=A_{D,\\rm dyn}(t-t_{0})\\Theta(t-t_{0})\\,, (C31) where A_{\\rm\\;D,dyn} is the dimensionless amplitude given by

A_{D,\\rm dyn}=\\frac{2GM}{\\tau v^{2}}\\hat{\\mathbf{d}}\\cdot\\hat{\\mathbf{b}}_{D}\\,. (C32)

Given the expressions in Eqs. (C26) and (C27) for the Doppler and Shapiro signals, we use the MC developed in Lee et al. (2021) to derive the distributions p(\\log_{10}A_{I}|f_{\\rm PBH}). Specifically, we proceed as follows:

\\bullet For each pulsar we generate a population of N_{\\rm PBH} PBHs, where N_{\\rm PBH} is implicitly defined by the relation

N_{\\rm PBH}=f_{\\rm PBH}\\frac{\\rho_{\\rm DM}V}{M_{\\rm PBH}}\\,, (C33) where the simulation volume, V, is a sphere of radius R=\\bar{v}T_{\\rm obs} centered around the pulsar position for the Doppler signal and a cylinder with the same radius and height given by the Earth-pulsar distance for the Shapiro signal. Here \\bar{v}\\simeq 340\\,\\rm km/s is the average PBH velocity, and T_{\\rm obs,\\it I} is the observation time of the Ith pulsar.

\\bullet For each of these PBHs we generate a random initial position and velocity. Since PTA searches are only sensitive to DM subhalos in the neighborhood of the solar system, we expect the position distribution to be uniform. Therefore, we use the probability density function f(\\mathbf{r})=\\mathbf{1}/\\mathbf{V} to sample initial positions. To sample PBHs' velocity, we use a Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution with v_{0}=325\\,\\rm kms^{-1}, v_{\\rm esc}=600\\,\\rm kms^{-1}, and the angular dependence assumed to be isotropic.

\\bullet The PBHs' signals are then classified as dynamic if they satisfy the condition T_{\\rm obs,\\it I}-\\tau>t_{0}>\\tau, and static otherwise.

\\bullet To evaluate A_{\\rm stat}, we sum the static signals of all PBHs computed by using Eqs. (C26) and (C27), and we fit the resulting signal to a cubic polynomial in time to extract the t^{3} term. To compute A_{\\rm D,dyn}, we take the closest transiting object and compute the signal amplitudes using Eq. (C32).

All the previous points are repeated for 2.5\\times 10^{3} realizations to obtain the distributions p(\\log_{10}A_{I}|f_{\\rm PBH}). Given the conditional distributions p(\\log_{10}A_{I}|f_{\\rm PBH}) and the posterior distribution p(\\log_{10}A_{I}|\\mathbf{\\delta t}) derived by analyzing our data, we can write

p\\left(f_{\\rm PBH}|\\mathbf{\\delta t}\\right)=\\prod_{I=1}^{N_{P}}\\int p(f_{\\rm PBH }|\\log_{10}A_{I})p\\left(\\log_{10}A_{I}|\\mathbf{\\delta t}\\right)d\\log_{10}A_{I}\\,. (C34)

Then, using Bayes' theorem, we can rewrite

p\\left(f_{\\rm PBH}|\\log_{10}A_{I}\\right)=\\frac{p\\left(\\log_{10}A_{I}|f_{\\rm PBH }\\right)p\\left(f_{\\rm PBH}\\right)}{p\\left(\\log_{10}A_{I}\\right)}\\,. (C35)

Our priors on p\\left(f_{\\rm PBH}\\right) and p\\left(\\log_{10}A_{I}\\right) are uniform, which allows us to eventually write Eq. (C34) as

p\\left(f_{\\rm PBH}|\\mathbf{\\delta t}\\right)\\propto\\prod_{I=1}^{N_{P}}\\int p(\\log_{ 10}A_{I}|f_{\\rm PBH})p(\\log_{10}A_{I}|\\mathbf{\\delta t})\\ d\\log_{10}A_{I}\\,, (C36) where the \\propto implies that the p(f_{\\rm PBH}|\\mathbf{\\delta t}) would be subject to the normalization condition, \\int_{0}^{\\infty}p(f_{\\rm PBH}|\\mathbf{\\delta t})\\ df_{\\rm PBH}=1.

In the presence of a DM-baryon fifth force in the form of a Yukawa potential in Eq. (72), assuming point-mass DM, the pulsar frequency shift due to Doppler effect is given by (Gresham et al., 2023)

\\left(\\frac{\\delta\\nu}{\\nu}\\right)_{\\rm fifth}=\\frac{\\tilde{\\alpha}GM}{\\tau_{D}^{ 2}v}\\hat{d}\\cdot\\int\\frac{1}{(1+x_{D}^{2})^{3/2}}\\left(1+\\frac{b}{\\lambda} \\sqrt{1+x_{D}^{2}}\\right)e^{-(b/\\lambda)\\sqrt{1+x_{D}^{2}}}(\\hat{b}+x_{D}\\hat{ v})dx_{D}\\,. (C37)

The integral in Eq. (C37) has to be computed numerically, and the signal due to the fifth force can be computed by performing an additional integration over time and subtracting away degeneracies with timing model parameters. The total signal is the sum of the gravitational and the fifth-force contribution, h_{D,\\,\\rm total}(t)=h_{D,\\,\\rm fifth}(t)+h_{D(S)}(t). In this analysis of the fifth-foe constraint, we only consider the scenario where the DM substructure makes up the entirety of the DM local density, which is equivalent to taking f_{\\rm PBH}=1 for the gravitational contribution. Parameterizing the signal as h_{\\rm D,\\,total}(t)=\\frac{A_{D,\\,\\rm total}}{y^{2}}t^{3} similar to the PBH case, the amplitude A_{D,\\,\\rm total} is a random variable dependent on \\lambda and \\tilde{\\alpha}. The probability distribution function P(\\log_{10}A_{D,\\,\\rm total}|\\lambda,\\tilde{\\alpha}) can be extracted again by Monte Carlo simulations and Bayes' theorem. Finally, the posterior distribution of \\tilde{\\alpha} and \\lambda, P(\\tilde{\\alpha},\\lambda|\\boldsymbol{\\delta t}), is given by the analog of Eq. (C36)

p\\left(\\tilde{\\alpha},\\lambda|\\boldsymbol{\\delta t}\\right)\\propto\\prod_{I=1}^{ N_{P}}\\int p(\\log_{10}A_{I}|\\tilde{\\alpha},\\lambda)p(\\log_{10}A_{I}|\\boldsymbol{ \\delta t})\\;d\\log_{10}A_{I}\\,. (C38)